<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · monument</p><h1 class="enc-title">Chepstow Castle</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1067</span> · <a href="/centuries/11th-century">11th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Chepstow Castle stands on a cliff above the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/river-wye">River Wye</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/monmouthshire">Monmouthshire</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wales">Wales</a>. Begun by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-fitzosbern">William FitzOsbern</a> in 1067, it is the oldest surviving post-Roman stone fortification in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> and was the base for Norman expansion into Gwent; it was later enlarged by the Marshal and Bigod families.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/chepstow-castle.jpg" alt="Chepstow Castle" width="320" height="212" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Nilfanion · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Chepstow_Castle_(4197).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>The castle stands on a narrow ridge between the limestone river cliff and a valley known locally as the Dell, overlooking an important crossing of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/river-wye">River Wye</a> on the routes inland to Monmouth and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hereford">Hereford</a>. Its four baileys, added one after another, record its building history rather than make it strong: it has neither a strong keep nor a concentric plan. It was first called Striguil, from the Welsh ystraigl, river bend, and with its Marcher lordship took the name of the neighbouring market town of Chepstow from the late 14th century.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The Great Tower, probably finished by about 1090, was built in stone from the start, partly with blocks reused from the Roman ruins at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caerwent">Caerwent</a>. In the 12th century the castle served the conquest of Gwent, the first independent Welsh kingdom the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/normans">Normans</a> conquered. William Marshal, Earl of Pembroke, built the present main gatehouse from the 1190s and strengthened the Middle Bailey with round towers. Roger Bigod, 5th Earl of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/norfolk">Norfolk</a>, who inherited it in 1270, added a new range in the Lower Bailey, the town's Port Wall around 1274–78, and the tower later called Marten's Tower.</p><p>Its defensive value declined after the wars between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wales">Wales</a> ended. Garrisoned with twenty men-at-arms and sixty <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/archery">archers</a> against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/owain-glyndwr">Owain Glyndŵr</a> in 1403, it was not attacked. Sir Charles Somerset, later Earl of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/worcester">Worcester</a>, who received it in 1508, remodelled it as a residence. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/english-civil-war">English Civil War</a> the Royalist garrison was besieged in 1645 and 1648, and the castle fell to Parliament on 25 May 1648; Sir Nicholas Kemeys, who led the defence, was killed after refusing to surrender. It then served as an artillery fort, barracks and political prison, where Henry Marten, a signatory of the death warrant of Charles I, was held until his death in 1680.</p><p>The garrison was disbanded in 1685 and the buildings left to decay, used in part as a farmyard and a glass factory. By the late 18th century the ruins were a stop on the Wye tour, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/j-m-w-turner">J. M. W. Turner</a> painted the castle in 1794. The film Ivanhoe was made in its grounds in 1913, and the following year the businessman William Royse Lysaght bought it and began conservation.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The ruins were listed Grade I on 6 December 1950, and the Lysaght family placed the castle in the care of the Ministry of Works in 1953. Since 1984 it has been looked after by Cadw, the Welsh government's heritage body, and it is open to the public.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Chepstow Castle” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>After the Norman Conquest William FitzOsbern built <b>Chepstow Castle</b> from 1067 and pushed west.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-gwent">Kingdom of Gwent</a></li></ul></section>
